WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:06.965
Welcome back! In the last video, we
explored the National Data tab and looked

2
00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:10.747
at the demographics of people
with and without disabilities.

3
00:00:10.747 --> 00:00:14.278
In this video, we will continue
exploring national data,

4
00:00:14.278 --> 00:00:16.674
and we will focus on community living.

5
00:00:16.674 --> 00:00:19.889
Let's scroll to the top and
click on national data.

6
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.209
Under Category, click on Community Living.

7
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.598
There are two types of living categories.

8
00:00:26.598 --> 00:00:30.775
There's community living, and
there's institutionalized living.

9
00:00:30.775 --> 00:00:34.156
Living in a community
includes two types of people.

10
00:00:34.156 --> 00:00:38.730
People who live at home, or in a
non-institutionalized group quarter.

11
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.339
We will first explore the home category.

12
00:00:41.339 --> 00:00:45.401
Home is the preferred place for
people with disabilities to live.

13
00:00:45.401 --> 00:00:48.650
This is considered a positive
outcome, a good thing.

14
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:52.399
We want to see more people with
disabilities living at home.

15
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.012
So, as we learned before,
when we look at the map,

16
00:00:56.012 --> 00:00:59.342
the darker colors mean more
something, more opportunity.

17
00:00:59.342 --> 00:01:03.206
This means that more people with
disabilities are living at home,

18
00:01:03.206 --> 00:01:04.930
which is what we want to see.

19
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.273
The bottom of this map tells you
exactly what these colors mean.

20
00:01:09.273 --> 00:01:13.349
When looking at a key, it is important
to look at the percentage ranges.

21
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.784
This key shows that 87.5
to 97% of people with

22
00:01:16.784 --> 00:01:21.920
disabilities live at home in the
United States. This is a good thing.

23
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.121
This means that the
majority of people with

24
00:01:25.121 --> 00:01:28.619
disabilities in the United
States live at home.

25
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.440
When we look at the southern region of the
US, we can see a lot of darker colors.

26
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.157
This means a lot of
people in the South are

27
00:01:37.157 --> 00:01:42.340
living with a disability and are living at
home. Do you notice any other patterns?

28
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.067
When we scroll down to the, below
the map, we can see the data table.

29
00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:49.896
So, as before, we can see
on the left-hand side,

30
00:01:49.896 --> 00:01:54.080
it shows you the states, territories,
and the United States as a whole.

31
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.353
Then, it shows you the
number and the percentage of

32
00:01:57.353 --> 00:02:00.039
people with disabilities
who are living at home.

33
00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.885
Next to that, it tells you
the number and percentage

34
00:02:03.885 --> 00:02:07.369
of people without disabilities
who are living at home.

35
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.810
Let's scroll to the top and select
non-institutionalized Group Quarters.

36
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:19.663
Non-institutionalized group quarters
include residencies like group homes,

37
00:02:19.663 --> 00:02:22.489
missions, college dorms, or shelters.

38
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.900
This is kind of a neutral topic.
It's neither good nor bad.

39
00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.647
It's better than living in an institution,

40
00:02:29.647 --> 00:02:33.150
but it might not be someone's
preferred site to live at.

41
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.959
When we look at the map, in the key.

42
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.601
We can see that the darker colors
still mean more of something,

43
00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:44.109
so more people who live in a
non-institutionalized group quarter.

44
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.605
We can see that in states like California,

45
00:02:46.605 --> 00:02:49.082
they have a higher number
of people who live

46
00:02:49.082 --> 00:02:51.560
in the non-institutionalized
group quarters.

47
00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.963
Comparatively, in the southern region,

48
00:02:53.963 --> 00:02:58.830
there's a lot more lighter colored states,
so less people living in these groups.

49
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.702
When looking at the map, it's important
to understand what these colors mean.

50
00:03:03.702 --> 00:03:06.001
When looking at the percentage range,

51
00:03:06.001 --> 00:03:09.230
the percentage of people
with disabilities living in

52
00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.460
group quarters in the US is
quite low across states.

53
00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:16.635
So, not many people are living
in non-institutionalized

54
00:03:16.635 --> 00:03:20.650
group quarters for people
with or without disabilities.

55
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:21.980
However.

56
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.522
As we learned before, we need to
look at the data table to understand

57
00:03:26.522 --> 00:03:29.149
how many people this actually represents.

58
00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.720
So let's scroll down.

59
00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.913
Let's look at the United
States, for example.

60
00:03:34.913 --> 00:03:38.024
It shows that 2.2% of
people with disabilities

61
00:03:38.024 --> 00:03:41.068
live in non-institutionalized
group quarters.

62
00:03:41.068 --> 00:03:45.599
This still represents more than a
million people with disabilities.

63
00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.626
We have explored both
categories of community living,

64
00:03:51.626 --> 00:03:55.552
living at home and living in a
non-institutionalized group quarter.

65
00:03:55.552 --> 00:03:58.013
Now, we're going to explore data on people

66
00:03:58.013 --> 00:04:00.650
with disabilities who
live in an institution.

67
00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:02.709
Click on Topic.

68
00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.900
And click on Nursing Home, Ages 18 to 64.

69
00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.176
Living in an institution includes nursing
homes and correctional facilities.

70
00:04:13.176 --> 00:04:15.965
We will start by exploring
nursing home data

71
00:04:15.965 --> 00:04:18.500
for people between the ages of 18 to 64.

72
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:23.238
Living in a nursing home is, for
some, considered a bad outcome.

73
00:04:23.238 --> 00:04:27.700
We want to see more people with
disabilities living in the community.

74
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.150
Before, we saw that the darker the color,

75
00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:35.539
it means more opportunity, which
was considered a good thing.

76
00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:41.130
This is still true, but the darker
colors represent, in this map.

77
00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:46.408
fewer people who are living in a nursing
home, which is what we want to see.

78
00:04:46.408 --> 00:04:49.400
So we still want to see
those darker colors,

79
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:54.500
and it still represents more opportunity,
it just shows a lower percentage.

80
00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:57.280
So when we look at the map.

81
00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.197
The Midwest region has
a higher percentage of

82
00:05:00.197 --> 00:05:05.039
people with disabilities living in nursing
homes, so you might wonder why that is.

83
00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:09.350
To find more specific information, you
can scroll down to the data table.

84
00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.409
Generally, looking at this table,

85
00:05:13.409 --> 00:05:18.394
I can see that more people with disabilities
between the ages of 18 to 64 live

86
00:05:18.394 --> 00:05:23.060
in a nursing home than people without
disabilities in the same age group.

87
00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.783
However, in general, these
are very low percentages,

88
00:05:26.783 --> 00:05:29.339
which is overall a really good thing.

89
00:05:29.339 --> 00:05:32.863
We want to see more people
living in the community.

90
00:05:32.863 --> 00:05:37.700
Alright, let's scroll back up to the
top and explore the next section.

91
00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.649
Click on Correctional Facility.

92
00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:47.160
This map shows us people with disabilities
who live in a correctional facility.

93
00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.381
So, just like the nursing home,

94
00:05:49.381 --> 00:05:54.570
we want to see fewer people with disabilities
living in a correctional facility.

95
00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:57.960
We still want to see darker
colors, but this time,

96
00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.209
the darker colors represent
the lower percentages.

97
00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.408
We can see that at the
bottom, there's a key,

98
00:06:04.408 --> 00:06:09.079
and we can see that these percentages are
relatively low, which is a good thing.

99
00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:14.300
We can see a lot of the states in the
West Coast have this darker color.

100
00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:18.288
You might wonder why there's a
difference in the West Coast,

101
00:06:18.288 --> 00:06:22.422
kind of pretty distinctly from the
South, compared to the Midwest.

102
00:06:22.422 --> 00:06:26.869
You can scroll down below to the data
table and compare the information

103
00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.211
For people with disabilities and without

104
00:06:29.211 --> 00:06:31.820
disabilities living in a
correctional facility.

105
00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:37.789
You can see that for some states,
let's choose Illinois, for example.

106
00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:41.506
The percentage of people
living in a nursing

107
00:06:41.506 --> 00:06:44.590
home for people with
disabilities is nearly

108
00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:50.291
double the percentage of people without
disabilities living in a nursing home.

109
00:06:50.291 --> 00:06:56.211
So in Illinois, 0.7% of people with
disabilities live in a correctional facility,

110
00:06:56.211 --> 00:06:59.719
compared to 0.4% of people
without disabilities.

111
00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.740
This is an interesting point to explore.

112
00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.374
The next two categories are
related to housing access.

113
00:07:08.374 --> 00:07:11.999
So go back to the top, and
under Topic, click on…

114
00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:15.459
HCV, Disabled Head of Household.

115
00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:21.479
This map shows the number of people
with disabilities who receive

116
00:07:21.479 --> 00:07:27.029
federal rental assistance through
the Housing Choice Voucher program.

117
00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:31.214
Specifically, it shows the
percentages of people received,

118
00:07:31.214 --> 00:07:34.909
who receive HCVs who are the
head of their household.

119
00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:40.557
This map shows a pretty interesting divide
between the West Coast and East Coast.

120
00:07:40.557 --> 00:07:42.300
Why do you think that is?

121
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.589
Let's go to the last category, Public
Housing Disabled Head of Household.

122
00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:54.127
This last category shows the
percentage of households in the state

123
00:07:54.127 --> 00:07:58.535
living in public housing whose head
of household has a disability.

124
00:07:58.535 --> 00:08:03.209
If you want to, explore more of the
information, you can scroll below.

125
00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:04.989
And look at the data table.

126
00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.520
All right. We have
explored community living,

127
00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.681
so living in a home or a
non-institutionalized group quarter.

128
00:08:13.681 --> 00:08:16.409
We've explored institutionalized living,

129
00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:20.707
such as living in a nursing home
or in a correctional facility.

130
00:08:20.707 --> 00:08:24.390
We've also explored different
types of housing access.

131
00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:27.746
If you want to download
any of this information,

132
00:08:27.746 --> 00:08:32.060
you can click on the download button
on the top right corner of the page.

133
00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:33.774
That concludes today's video.

134
00:08:33.774 --> 00:08:37.910
Stay tuned for the next session on
community participation. Thank you!
